  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMid-level ridging is in place over the central CONUS with a  \ntrough digging into the western CONUS. Hurricane Isaias has made\nlandfall along the Gulf Coast and will be absorbed into the \npattern as a shortwave trough over the next couple of days. \nWhile the remnants of Isaias will stay to the east of the area, \nthe absorption into the pattern will keep the mid-level ridging \ncentered over the lower Missouri River Valley over the next \nseveral days. At the surface, a low pressure system is moving \neast along the Canadian border. The pressure fields surrounding \nthe low have induced southerly flow across much of the Plains. \nThis southerly flow, in conjunction with the upper-level ridge, \nwill continue to support well above normal temperatures for \nearly-to-mid October by 15-20 degrees. Highs will largely be in \nthe mid-to-upper 80s today through Monday with areas along and \nsouth of I-70 potentially pushing above 90F. This forecast \nbrings record high temperatures into play on Sunday and Monday \nat KMCI (89F in 1962 and 91F in 1899 respectively) and KSTJ (89F\nin 1989 and 2024 and 92F in 1975 respectively).\n\nAs has been well advertised in previous forecast cycles, a good \ndeal of uncertainty enters the forecast on Tuesday regarding \nprecipitation and temperatures. Model guidance continues to be \nsplit on how quickly moisture associated with the remnants of \nHurricane Simon reaches the Central Plains. Faster solutions \n(GFS and GEFS) present lower high temperatures and higher \nchances for precipitation Tuesday afternoon, while slower \nsolutions (ECMWF and ENS) favor a continuation of well above \nnormal highs and a dry forecast. LREF quartile spreads have \nnarrowed slightly from this time yesterday and are now on the \norder of 10 degrees between the 25th and 75th percentiles (76F \nand 87F respectively in the KC Metro).\n\nRegardless of faster or slower solution, precipitation chances \nincrease on Wednesday to 60-75%. This is due to a trough over \nthe western CONUS ejecting eastward and inciting lee \ncyclogenesis in eastern CO. A warm front will lift north on \nWednesday bringing a source of lift to the area in conjunction \nwith upper-level support from the shortwave. How far north the \nwarm front makes it on Wednesday will be dictated by how far \nsouth the upper-level tough digs before ejecting eastward, which\nwill drive the location of lee cyclogenesis. Areas south of the\nwarm front will see 500-1000 J/kg of MUCAPE build by Wednesday \nafternoon with 40-50 kts of deep layer shear creating a classic \nhigh shear, low CAPE setup for potential strong to severe storms\nWednesday evening/night. Though we will have to wait a couple \nmore hours for the next SPC outlook (around 09Z), there is \ncurrently a Day 6 Slight (Level 2 of 5) Risk across the southern\nportion of the CWA (including the KC Metro) for Wednesday.\n\nAs the trough and low pressure system advance east, a cold front \nwill push through the area on Thursday, which should bring Fall \ntemperatures back into the forecast by the end of next week.",
